window.docContents[29].resolve({"/#inductive-type-universes":{"contents":"Structures and inductive datatypes can be declared to inhabit particular universes.\nLean then checks whether each datatype avoids paradoxes by being in a universe that's large enough to prevent it from containing its own type.\nFor instance, in the following declaration,  is declared to reside in , and so is its type argument :\n\n\n\n itself is a .\nThis means that it cannot be used to contain actual types, because then its argument would be , which is a :\n\n\n\n\n\nUpdating  so that its argument is a  results in a definition rejected by Lean:\n\n\n\n\n\nThis error occurs because the argument to  with type  is from a larger universe than .\nPlacing  itself in  solves this issue, but at the cost of  now being itself inconvenient to use in contexts that expect a .\n\nThe specific rules that govern whether a datatype is allowed are somewhat complicated.\nGenerally speaking, it's easiest to start with the datatype in the same universe as the largest of its arguments.\nThen, if Lean rejects the definition, increase its level by one, which will usually go through.\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Functors, Applicative Functors, and Monads\u0009Universes","header":"5.5.1. User Defined Types","id":"/#inductive-type-universes"}});